1.What is rendering in React?
Rendering in React is the process of converting a component’s state and props into UI elements (React elements) and then updating the DOM to reflect that UI.
Browser HTML rendering directly parses HTML and updates the DOM immediately, while React rendering first creates a Virtual DOM, compares changes using reconciliation, 
and updates only the necessary parts of the real DOM

2.What happens when a React component renders?
When a React component renders, React calls the component function, creates a Virtual DOM representation of the UI, 
compares it with the previous render, and updates only the necessary parts of the real DOM.

When a React component renders, React executes the component, builds a new Virtual DOM tree,
reconciles it with the previous tree, and commits only the minimal necessary changes to the real DOM.

3.What triggers a re-render in React?
A React component re-renders when its state, props, or context change, or when its parent re-renders, causing React to re-execute the component function.

4.what  ia props?
Props are immutable inputs passed from parent to child that determine a component’s rendered output, 
and any change in props triggers a re-render of the receiving component.
5.props vs state?
Props are immutable inputs provided by a parent, while state is mutable data owned by the component. 
Both affect rendering, but props control configuration and state controls behavior over time.

6.What is the difference between initial render and re-render?
Initial render is when a component is rendered for the first time and its DOM is created,
while a re-render is when React re-executes the component due to data changes and updates only the parts of the DOM that changed.

Performance difference
Initial render ?
More expensive
DOM creation
Happens once per mount

Re-render
Usually cheaper
DOM diffing + selective updates
Can happen many times


7.What is reconciliation in React?
Reconciliation is the process by which React compares the new Virtual DOM tree with the previous one to determine the minimal set of changes needed to update the real DOM.

8.What is Virtual DOM in React?
Virtual DOM is an in-memory JavaScript representation of the UI that allows React to efficiently diff changes and update only the necessary parts of the real DOM.

9.What is React Fiber?
React Fiber is React’s internal rendering architecture that breaks rendering work into small units so updates can be paused,
prioritized, and resumed, enabling concurrent rendering and better performance.
React Fiber was introduced to make rendering interruptible and priority-based, so React can keep the UI responsive even in large, complex applications.

Why Fiber was needed ?
React needed to:
Break rendering into small units
Pause work to handle urgent tasks
Resume later without restarting
Assign priority levels to updates

How does Fiber improve rendering performance?
React Fiber improves rendering performance by breaking rendering work into small units,
prioritizing updates, and allowing rendering to pause, resume, or discard work to keep the UI responsive.

10.How does Server-Side Rendering (SSR) change the rendering flow and CSR client side rendering?
Server-Side Rendering (SSR) changes the rendering flow by rendering React components to HTML on the server first, 
sending that HTML to the browser, and then “hydrating” it so React takes control on the client.

CSR Flow
Browser requests page
Server sends empty HTML + JS bundle
Browser downloads JS
React renders Virtual DOM → creates DOM
UI appears

SSR Flow
Browser requests page
Server renders React components → generates HTML string
HTML sent to browser → page visible immediately
Browser downloads JS → hydrates HTML → page becomes interactive

CSR renders React components entirely in the browser leading to slower initial load but fast navigation,
while SSR renders HTML on the server for faster first paint and better SEO, followed by client-side hydration to enable interactivity.
SSR is always better than CSR”
✔ Depends on SEO needs, performance, and app type

❌ “CSR is dead”
✔ CSR is perfect for apps with lots of client interactivity


what is a hydrating in react ?
Hydration in React is the process of attaching React’s event listeners and internal state to the HTML 
that was pre-rendered on the server (SSR) so the page becomes fully interactive on the client
Why hydration exists
In Server-Side Rendering (SSR):
React components are rendered on the server → converted to HTML
The browser receives fully-rendered HTML → page visible immediately
BUT server-rendered HTML is static → buttons don’t work, forms are not interactive
React needs to “take over” the HTML → add event listeners, manage state
✅ This process is called hydration
